    <DataNode elementId="AT1G17290-MONOMER" textLabel="alanine transaminase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="AT1G17290-MONOMER" dataSource="plantcyc" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="275" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">Expression of AlaAT1 and AlaAT2 (AOAT4 and AOAT3, respectively) are induced under hypoxic conditions, oxygen stress. Comparison of the AlaAT1 knock-out mutants with wild type plants for alanine concentration changes over hypoxic stress and the subsequent recovery indicated that AlaAT1 is primarily involved in alanine breakdown during recovery from low oxygen stress. Alanine biosynthesis under hypoxia does not require AlaAT1 activity.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT1G17290-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-33137, RXN-7234" />
      <Property key="CatalyzesCount" value="2" />
      <Property key="GOTerms" value="GO:0005829" />
      <Property key="Accession1" value="AT1G17290.1" />
      <Property key="FunctionalAssignmentStatus" value=":PROBABLE-ENZYME" />
      <Property key="Gene" value="AT1G17290" />
      <Property key="^citations" value="17319845" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="MONOMER-*" />
      <Property key="Locations" value="CCI-CYTOSOL-PL" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_17319845" />
    </DataNode>
    <DataNode elementId="AT1G70580-MONOMER" textLabel="Glutamate--glyoxylate aminotransferase 2" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9S7E9" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="275" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">In vitro assay of the recombinant proteins indicated that GGAT1 and GGAT2 have four aminotransferase activities, namely GGAT, AGAT, GPAT and AOAT. The two recombinant proteins exhibited very similar Km values towards amino acid substrates glutamate and alanine, as well as the oxoacid substrates glyoxylate, pyruvate and 2-oxoglutarate|CITS: [12529529]|. AOAT2 (GGAT2), which is predicted to be located in the peroxisome, is likely involved in photorespiration.</Comment>
